## STEP 2 — CONTRACT CONFORMANCE, RESULTS

**A. Never-silent receipt / final-claim rule — 180/180 on my own matrix.** 20 fabricated completions ×
{claims:null, claims:[], state-only} = 60, plus 20 trailing-question + `pendingAction` variants: every one
ends in the deterministic receipt with zero success wording surviving and `receiptRendered: true`.
20 truthful READ answers × 3 claim shapes = 60: **all survive verbatim**. 20 turns with a VERIFIED
envelope: the truthful claim renders. 20 with executed-but-unverified envelopes: **none supports a claim.**
Structural mutants confirm this is not vacuous — disabling the receipt block, re-adding the
`!result.pendingAction` exemption, letting the receipt fire without intent, ignoring the postcondition,
and re-introducing the belt fallback are **all killed**. *Caveat: this covers only commands the intent
tier recognises — V64-D1 is the hole.*

**B. Request-intent derivation.** `requestedIntent === requestedIntentPrimary`; derived only from the
model's `requestIntent`, the model action arrays, confirmation shape and the request lexicon. Never from
`result.summary`, tense, the belt or `pendingAction` — the mutants that re-introduce the belt fallback and
the model's `kind:'read'` veto are both killed. Attack results in V64-D1.

**C. Server-side lifecycle resolution.** `company_lifecycle_matrix` 28/0 and `v56`–`v63` all pass. The
resolver queries `companies` with no status filter; for `restore` it prefers the archived row when an
exact name matches one active and one archived; a fuzzy hit from the raw command **asks and never
executes**; a model-emitted fuzzy hit executes only when unique; disambiguation arms a `pendingAction`
and executes nothing. `deleteTaskIds` / `pendingDeleteTaskIds` remain window-gated by design and I agree
that is correct — but see V64-D0: the gate itself has no behavioural test.

**D. Collection envelopes.** Totals come from `{ count: 'exact' }`, never array length; `companies` splits
active/archived newest-first; every array in the pack literal has an envelope. Exception: `namedTargets`
(V64-D5). Minor: `collections.companies.shown` can exceed `total` when the founder names an archived
company, since `total` counts non-archived rows only.

**E/F/G.** `lifecycle_evidence_and_output_persistence_contract`, `mutation_receipt_equals_ledger`,
`architecture_mutation_envelope_contract`, `grounding_precedence_canonical_over_history` all pass.
Durable-row precedence is correct (§2 tier 3 over tier 4), TTL-guarded and fully-typed; the stored-output
fallback carries its own 30-minute expiry. I could not make a stale durable row execute anything — the
plan's ids are re-validated against provenance + `namedTargets` and the RPCs re-read.

**H. Belt as defence-in-depth.** With **no** intent the belt rewrites nothing — 60/60 truthful read
answers survived verbatim. `belt_generative_adversarial_contract`, `v92_parity_contract` and
`v92_open_regression_contract` all pass. This is `INTEGRATION VERIFIED`; I did not run an independent
300-negative / 200-fabrication corpus, because the intent-tier P1 makes the belt's measured delta the
lesser question this round.

